# Netlib Kennington subset — 16 instances, pinned.
#
# Sixteen larger problems from Carolan, Hill, Kennington, Niemi and Wichmann,
# "An Empirical Evaluation of the KORBX Algorithms for Military Airlift
# Applications", Operations Research 38(2), 1990, 240-248. The M1 gate wants
# them for correctness only, with no performance expectation (PLAN 2.9).
#
#   make netlib-kennington
#
# Distributed at netlib.org/lp/data/kennington as gzipped packed files.
# bench/fetch.sh gunzips and then expands them with netlib's emps, which it
# downloads and checksum-verifies at fetch time and never stores here
# (PLAN 2.10, Q6).
#
#     name  sha256  rows  cols  reference  source  objconst
#
# The sha256 is of the .gz exactly as netlib serves it.
#
# rows/cols are counted from the expanded MPS: constraint rows only, N rows
# excluded, distinct COLUMNS names. Cross-checked against the statistics
# table in that directory's readme, which agrees on every column count and
# gives one more row throughout — the objective row, which it counts and
# JAOS does not. That is the same offset the standard set showed against the
# canonical PROBLEM SUMMARY TABLE.
#
# reference comes from the same readme, where the optima were computed with
# Vanderbei's ALPO. They carry eight significant digits. The gate's tolerance
# is 1e-6 relative, so there is room — but these are not Koch's exact
# rationals, and an instance that misses by less than 1e-8 relative cannot be
# judged against them. Marked `netlib` in the source column for that reason.
# Whether Koch's results cover these is still unverified (bench/README.md).
#
# These are much larger than anything else JAOS loads: ken-18 has 105127 rows
# and 154699 columns, osa-60 has 232966 columns. This set is the first real
# test of whether the readers and the factorization scale past the standard
# set at all.
#
